  set:  # eade
    title: "agda: layout blocks nested in a module (non-empty nested module, record, data; an empty nested module followed by top-level code) lose scope chains or the enclosing module under tree-sitter-agda 1.3.3"  # eade
    description: "[WIDENED 2026-11-15 by owner ruling, dev e2b6ab8096, tree-sitter-agda 1.3.3 as bundled]\nThe trigger is wider than the original title's \"empty nested module body\". Under the bundled grammar, layout blocks\nnested inside a module lose scope chains; an empty nested module ALONE is handled correctly.\n\nProbe grid (each a file `module T where` followed by the shape; emitted names shown; fixtures and scripts in\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/stable_id_items_review_11152026/work/WI-molon):\n\n  shape                                            emitted                                         correct would be\n  non-empty nested module, last in file             A, A.C, A.c                                     A.C.c\n  non-empty nested module, then a sibling           A, A.C, A.c, run                                A.C.c, A.run\n  nested module with 3 declarations, then sibling   C, C.c, C.d (A lost)                            A, A.C, A.C.c, A.C.d\n  empty nested module, then `run` in A              A, A.C, A.run                                   (correct)\n  empty nested module, then top-level code          C, run, B, B.f (A lost)  <- the filed shape     A, A.C, A.run, B, B.f\n  empty nested module, last in A, then top-level    run, C, B, B.f (A lost)                         A, A.run, A.C, B, B.f\n  nested module then a top-level module             A, A.C, A.C.c, A.C.B, A.f                       B, B.f at top level\n  nested record inside a module, then sibling       A, A.R, A.R.x, run                              A.run\n  nested data (two constructors), then sibling      A, A.D, A.k1, A.k2, run, B, f                   A.run, B.f (run and f lose their module)\n\ntree-sitter-language-pack 0.13.0 parses all 16 probes with has_error False. The decision between upgrading or\npatching the grammar and working around it in the analyzer is the item's open step; WI-vimof holds the corpus\ncensus (747 of 1,247 agda-stdlib files flagged by root.has_error under 1.3.3, 510 under the pack).\n\nNot reproduced, and to be removed from the analyzer's docstring: the claim at agda.py:65-69 that only the first\nclause-level where block nests under its clause. In the three probes run (W, W2, Cl) every where block nests under its own clause.\n\nAcceptance: every row of the grid emits the \"correct would be\" names; the docstring claim is corrected.\n\n--- text before the widening ---\n[REVIEW 2026-11-01, PARTLY-STALE] Reviewed 2026-11-01 on dev 9ae4abe13f (tree-sitter-agda 1.3.3, fx_molon). Part 1 reproduces but is INV-vokir's (P3) mechanism: after a one-constructor data block the next signature splits into two function nodes (foo not emitted), and a following 'data Bool' header is lost too. It does not swallow every later declaration. Part 2 as titled does NOT reproduce: in 4 where-block probes each block nests under its own clause. The F2 correction does reproduce: an empty nested 'module C where' gives an ERROR, enclosing module A is not emitted, and 'run' loses its A chain. agda.py:65-69 repeats the where claim. (Original text below; the review entry in the thread has the evidence.)\n\nReported by the WI-tusad B4 fix agent (PR #1562, 2026-10-12); not independently reproduced. Two tree-sitter-agda parse defects seen while migrating Agda stable_ids: (1) a data declaration with exactly one constructor is misparsed; (2) only the first clause-level where block in a file is nested under its clause, later ones are not. Neither was caused by B4. Effect on output not measured: symbols inside a mis-nested where may get the wrong scope chain (and so a different stable_id than intended). Next step: minimal repro for each, then decide grammar upgrade/patch vs analyzer-side workaround (see scripts/build-source-grammars if agda is built from source)."  # eade
